A pointer demand pins a joint template through the value/pointer bridge: *Key[int]
matches no result of MakeKV exactly, so its dual Key[int] is tried, pinning T = int; the
Val[string] demand pins U in the same cluster. One instantiation MakeKV[int, string]
supplies both, and *Key[int] rides the bridge into NewSink. Without the dual in the joint
step, T would lift into a phantom Key[T] output and *Key[int] would fall to the caller.
-- config --
import-path: example.com/app
package-name: app
output: plumb_gen.go
-- example.com/app/a.go --
package app

type Key[T any] struct{}
type Val[U any] struct{}
type Sink struct{}

//plumb:build
func MakeKV[T, U any]() (Key[T], Val[U]) { return Key[T]{}, Val[U]{} }

//plumb:build
func NewSink(k *Key[int], v Val[string]) *Sink { return nil }
